**Read:** [Propagation of distributions using Monte Carlo — JCGM 101](../../metrology/sources/GUM-Monte-Carlo.lingua#r_79V20QP3T2KZ6GD07SK5TGZ1J4), the full guide, numerical-control conditions and examples; [Numerical arrays and reproducible random streams — NumPy documentation](../sources/Numerical-and-Random-Contracts.lingua#r_22SKS09JTH3YF4244RX2FYWHNY), the chosen distributions, generators, and stream contracts; [Ten Simple Rules for Reproducible Computational Research — Sandve and colleagues](../sources/Reproducible-Research-Rules.lingua#r_5WNPQ4CBNZFM416E5TTNSKKPQG), randomness recommendations reconsidered against the actual implementation.

For independent uniform X on [0,1] and Y = X², E(Y) = 1/3 and var(Y) = 4/45. The standard error of the sample mean from N independent draws is √(4/(45N)). At N = 10,000 it is about 0.002981. This describes Monte Carlo variation of an estimated mean under the stated independence assumptions, not the standard deviation of Y itself and not an experimental measurement uncertainty.

Use the shared distribution-propagation Record's exact median and equal-tail interval to examine summaries beyond the mean. Quantile estimation has different numerical behavior, especially in tails. A stable-looking mean does not establish adequate precision for a tail probability. Correlated samples need an appropriate dependence analysis rather than substitution of the raw draw count into an independent-sample formula.

Preserve enough information to repeat one stream, but also use independent runs or another justified assessment to investigate numerical error. An identical rerun with the same seed establishes repeatability of that sequence; it supplies no additional independent information about convergence. A comparison of two independent implementations can expose a shared distribution or correlation-construction mistake only if its oracle and assumptions are examined.

**Work:** run the squared-uniform benchmark at increasing sizes, predict mean-error scales before simulation, and compare independent batches. Estimate the median and 95% equal-tail endpoints and examine their numerical variation. Then reproduce a correlated two-input benchmark from metrology, preserving the joint model and showing why sampling the marginals independently changes the answer.

For a published stochastic study, state whether the reproduction target is the same stream, a numerical summary within a justified tolerance, or a statistical conclusion under fresh draws. Report simulation settings and numerical-control evidence separately from the input distributions and physical model. A large draw count alone is insufficient evidence for either.

**Continue:** [Build a numerical validation argument across several kinds of evidence](A-numerical-validation-study.lingua#r_7EY9S6DXHFHSJAGA30W35A4ZD8).

